# PR-1: PR-1: Customer onboarding and KYC identity verification

**Story.** As a prospective bank customer, I want to register my identity details so that a compliance officer can verify my eligibility and allow me to open an account, while ensuring underage users, self-verifications, and unverified account openings are blocked and my personal data remains protected.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] RegisterCustomerRequest requires non-empty FullName, Email, NationalId, and DateOfBirth; empty or whitespace string fields throw ArgumentException.
- [ ] POST api/customers (authorized with Policies.Customer) registers the currently signed-in user using ICurrentUser.UserId as Customer.Id, ignoring any external user identifier, sets Status to KycStatus.Pending, sets RegisteredAt to IClock.UtcNow, persists to BankDbContext.Customers, and publishes CustomerRegistered(CustomerId, OccurredAt).
- [ ] If a Customer record already exists for ICurrentUser.UserId, ICustomerService.RegisterAsync throws InvalidOperationException.
- [ ] If the applicant is under 18 years old as of the date derived from IClock.UtcNow (including one day before their 18th birthday, whereas exactly 18 years old is accepted), ICustomerService.RegisterAsync throws ArgumentException.
- [ ] GET api/customers/me (authorized with Policies.Customer) returns the signed-in user's Customer record via ICustomerService.GetAsync or 404 Not Found if not registered.
- [ ] POST api/customers/{id}/verify and POST api/customers/{id}/reject (authorized with Policies.Compliance) invoke VerifyAsync and RejectAsync respectively; if the caller is not in the Compliance role or if ICurrentUser.UserId equals customerId (self-verification or self-rejection), they throw UnauthorizedAccessException.
- [ ] VerifyAsync and RejectAsync throw KeyNotFoundException when customerId does not exist in BankDbContext.Customers.
- [ ] Customer.Verify(officerId) and Customer.Reject(officerId) transition Status from KycStatus.Pending to KycStatus.Verified or KycStatus.Rejected, set DecidedBy to officerId, and publish CustomerVerified or CustomerRejected; calling either method when Status is not Pending throws InvalidOperationException.
- [ ] KycAccountOpeningRule implements IAccountOpeningRule and throws UnauthorizedAccessException if the customer is unknown or Customer.Status is not KycStatus.Verified.
- [ ] CustomersModule registers ICustomerService and KycAccountOpeningRule as IAccountOpeningRule in DI, and Program.cs registers CustomersModule while BankDbContext exposes DbSet<Customer> Customers.
- [ ] Customer.NationalId and Customer.DateOfBirth are annotated with [Pii], and neither NationalId nor DateOfBirth ever appears in domain events (CustomerRegistered, CustomerVerified, CustomerRejected), application logs, or exception/error messages.

## Out of scope
- Updating customer profile fields or re-submitting a registration after rejection.
- Listing, searching, or filtering pending KYC applications for compliance officers.
- External identity provider, sanctions/PEP screening, or document upload integrations.
- Automated email or SMS notifications on registration, verification, or rejection.
- Account creation endpoints, balance management, or any monetary fees/ledger postings (no money movement occurs in this PR).

## Risks
- Self-verification or privilege escalation if compliance role checks or caller-vs-target UserId comparisons are omitted or if user identifiers are accepted from request payloads.
- Regulatory breach from onboarding minors if age calculation uses system clock instead of IClock.UtcNow or mishandles leap-year birthdays (e.g., February 29).
- GDPR/privacy violation if NationalId or DateOfBirth leaks into domain events, exception messages, or HTTP/application logs.
- Unverified or rejected customers opening bank accounts if KycAccountOpeningRule is not wired as IAccountOpeningRule or fails open on missing customer records.
- Invalid KYC state transitions (such as verifying a previously rejected customer) if Customer.Status is not enforced via a private setter and domain guard methods.

## Public contract
```
namespace Bank.Modules.Customers.Domain:
  enum KycStatus { Pending, Verified, Rejected }
  sealed class Customer { string Id (the user id, primary key); string FullName; string Email; [Pii] string NationalId;
                          [Pii] DateOnly DateOfBirth; KycStatus Status { get; private set; } (starts Pending); string? DecidedBy;
                          DateTimeOffset RegisteredAt;
                          void Verify(string officerId); void Reject(string officerId) }   // only from Pending, else InvalidOperationException
  sealed record CustomerRegistered(string CustomerId, DateTimeOffset OccurredAt) : IDomainEvent
  sealed record CustomerVerified(string CustomerId, string VerifiedBy, DateTimeOffset OccurredAt) : IDomainEvent
  sealed record CustomerRejected(string CustomerId, string RejectedBy, DateTimeOffset OccurredAt) : IDomainEvent
namespace Bank.Modules.Customers.Application:
  sealed record RegisterCustomerRequest(string FullName, string Email, string NationalId, DateOnly DateOfBirth)
  interface ICustomerService {
    Task<Customer> RegisterAsync(RegisterCustomerRequest request, CancellationToken ct = default);  // for the signed-in user;
        // already registered -> InvalidOperationException; under 18 on IClock's date -> ArgumentException; empty fields -> ArgumentException
    Task<Customer> VerifyAsync(string customerId, CancellationToken ct = default);   // compliance officer only, never themselves -> UnauthorizedAccessException; unknown -> KeyNotFoundException
    Task<Customer> RejectAsync(string customerId, CancellationToken ct = default);   // same rules as VerifyAsync
    Task<Customer?> GetAsync(string customerId, CancellationToken ct = default); }
  sealed class CustomerService(BankDbContext db, ICurrentUser user, IClock clock, IEventBus bus) : ICustomerService
  sealed class KycAccountOpeningRule(BankDbContext db) : IAccountOpeningRule   // not Verified (or unknown) -> UnauthorizedAccessException
CustomersModule registers ICustomerService and KycAccountOpeningRule as IAccountOpeningRule.
BankDbContext gets: DbSet<Customer> Customers
HTTP: POST api/customers (register yourself), GET api/customers/me, POST api/customers/{id}/verify, POST api/customers/{id}/reject (Compliance policy)
```

## Allowed scope
- `src/Bank.Api/Modules/Customers/**`
- `src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs`
- `src/Bank.Api/Program.cs`

_Brief from the product owner:_ New customers register with their full name, e-mail, national identity number and date of birth. Customers must be at least 18. A compliance officer reviews each registration and verifies or rejects it; nobody can verify themselves. A customer who is not verified cannot open an account. National id and date of birth are personal data and must never appear in events, logs or messages.


ALLOWED SCOPE (glob): ['src/Bank.Api/Modules/Customers/**', 'src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs', 'src/Bank.Api/Program.cs']

CODING CONVENTIONS:
# Coding conventions for Nordbank (the Builder must follow these)

Stack: C# / ASP.NET Core Web API (.NET 8+), EF Core (SQLite), xUnit. No new NuGet packages.

## Layout — one folder per module, one folder per layer
src/Bank.Api/Modules/<Module>/
  Api/          controllers + DTOs            (HTTP layer)
  Application/  services + interfaces         (use cases)
  Domain/       entities, enums, events       (business rules)
  Data/         repositories (optional)       (database access)
  <Module>Module.cs   static class with AddXModule(this IServiceCollection): services.AddScoped<IThing, Thing>()
Register a new module in Program.cs above the "// MODULES:" line.
Add a new table as a DbSet in src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs above the "// TABLES:" line.
Each module already has a CONSTITUTION.md written by the humans who own it: follow its rules.

## Rules
- Namespaces follow folders: Bank.Modules.<Module>.<Layer>.
- Controllers: [ApiController], [Route("api/...")], and ALWAYS an [Authorize(Policy = Policies.X)] (Bank.Modules.Auth.Policies:
  Customer, Staff, LoanOfficer, Compliance, Admin). Roles are in Bank.Modules.Auth.Roles.
- Current user: inject Bank.Modules.Auth.Application.ICurrentUser (UserId, IsInRole). Never trust user ids from request bodies.
- Time: inject Bank.Shared.Time.IClock and use clock.UtcNow. Never call DateTime.Now / DateTimeOffset.UtcNow directly.
- Settings: inject Bank.Shared.Settings.BankSettings (DefaultCurrency, SupportedCurrencies, DailyTransferLimit).
- Money: decimal only. Round to cents with Math.Round(x, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero).
- Money moves ONLY through Bank.Modules.Ledger.Application.ILedger.PostTransferAsync(from, to, amount, reference),
  which returns the transfer id and works in ONE currency. The bank's own accounts:
  Bank.Modules.Ledger.Domain.BankAccounts.For(BankAccountKind.X, currency) with kinds
  LoanFunding, FeeIncome, FxClearing, DepositHolding, InterestExpense, Suspense (EUR, SEK, USD).
- Accounts: Bank.Modules.Accounts.Application.IAccountService (GetAsync, IsOwnerAsync, ForOwnerAsync). Never edit Account.Balance.
- State machines: an entity owns its status (private setter) and changes it only through methods that check the current state.
- Entities: public parameterless constructor and public settable properties (except a status with a private setter),
  so the database and tests can create them.
- Events: sealed records implementing Bank.Shared.Events.IDomainEvent (with DateTimeOffset OccurredAt), published with
  IEventBus.PublishAsync(...). Handlers implement IEventHandler<TEvent> and are registered in their module.
- Personal data properties get [Pii] (Bank.Shared). Never put personal data in events, logs or messages.
- Do not name a type "Ledger" outside the Ledger module (namespace clash).
- Keep files small; one public type per file unless they are tiny records.


