# PR-9: PR-9: Update BankSettings for Swedish launch (SEK default currency and 5,000 EUR daily transfer limit)

**Story.** As a bank product owner preparing for the Swedish launch, I want new accounts to default to SEK and the daily transfer limit for all customers to be reduced from 10,000 to 5,000 EUR equivalent so that the bank complies with Swedish market expectations and regulatory guidance.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] Bank.Shared.Settings.BankSettings.DefaultCurrency is updated to "SEK".
- [ ] Bank.Shared.Settings.BankSettings.DailyTransferLimit is updated to 5000m (decimal 5_000m).
- [ ] Bank.Shared.Settings.BankSettings.SupportedCurrencies remains completely unchanged.
- [ ] When an authorized user (per existing account creation policies) creates a new account without specifying a currency, the account is created with currency "SEK" derived from injected BankSettings.DefaultCurrency.
- [ ] When an authorized user creates a new account while explicitly specifying another supported currency from BankSettings.SupportedCurrencies, the account is created in that requested currency.
- [ ] Existing accounts keep their existing currencies and balances unchanged.
- [ ] For all customers, cumulative outgoing transfers within a single UTC day (determined via IClock.UtcNow) are capped at 5000.00m EUR equivalent, using decimal arithmetic rounded to 2 decimal places with MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero.
- [ ] If a customer initiates a transfer that causes their daily total in EUR equivalent to exceed 5000.00m (for example 5000.01m), the request is rejected with the existing limit-exceeded validation error and no transfer is posted via ILedger.PostTransferAsync.
- [ ] Transfers that keep the customer's daily total in EUR equivalent at or below 5000.00m succeed as before for authorized users.
- [ ] All existing authorization policies ([Authorize(Policy = Policies.X)]) and ICurrentUser ownership checks on account creation and transfers remain enforced without change.

## Out of scope
- Migrating or converting existing customer accounts from EUR or other currencies to SEK.
- Adding or removing currencies in BankSettings.SupportedCurrencies.
- Implementing per-customer, per-role, or database-driven dynamic overrides for DefaultCurrency or DailyTransferLimit.
- Changing FX rate calculation, rounding rules, or ledger posting logic.
- Reversing or auditing transfers made prior to this change under the previous 10,000 EUR daily limit.
- Adding new API endpoints, UI changes, or customer notifications regarding the Swedish launch.

## Risks
- Any module that hardcodes "EUR" or 10000m instead of reading injected BankSettings will silently violate the Swedish default or the 5,000 EUR regulatory transfer limit.
- Inconsistent decimal rounding (not using Math.Round(x, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero)) when evaluating non-EUR transfers such as SEK against the 5,000 EUR limit could allow transfers above the regulatory cap or wrongly reject transfers right at 5,000.00 EUR.
- Customers accustomed to the 10,000 EUR daily limit will have transfers between 5,000.01 and 10,000.00 EUR rejected once deployed, which could cause failed payments if downstream callers or tests assume a 10,000 limit.
- Existing automated tests or seed data that rely on DefaultCurrency being EUR without explicitly passing a currency may fail or create SEK accounts unexpectedly.

## Public contract
```
BankSettings: DefaultCurrency = "SEK"; DailyTransferLimit = 5_000m; SupportedCurrencies unchanged.
```

## Allowed scope
- `src/Bank.Api/Shared/Settings/BankSettings.cs`

_Brief from the product owner:_ We launch in Sweden next week. New accounts must default to SEK instead of EUR, and following the regulator's guidance the daily transfer limit drops from 10,000 to 5,000 (EUR equivalent) for all customers. Nothing else changes.


ALLOWED SCOPE (glob): ['src/Bank.Api/Shared/Settings/BankSettings.cs']

CODING CONVENTIONS:
# Coding conventions for Nordbank (the Builder must follow these)

Stack: C# / ASP.NET Core Web API (.NET 8+), EF Core (SQLite), xUnit. No new NuGet packages.

## Layout — one folder per module, one folder per layer
src/Bank.Api/Modules/<Module>/
  Api/          controllers + DTOs            (HTTP layer)
  Application/  services + interfaces         (use cases)
  Domain/       entities, enums, events       (business rules)
  Data/         repositories (optional)       (database access)
  <Module>Module.cs   static class with AddXModule(this IServiceCollection): services.AddScoped<IThing, Thing>()
Register a new module in Program.cs above the "// MODULES:" line.
Add a new table as a DbSet in src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs above the "// TABLES:" line.
Each module already has a CONSTITUTION.md written by the humans who own it: follow its rules.

## Rules
- Namespaces follow folders: Bank.Modules.<Module>.<Layer>.
- Controllers: [ApiController], [Route("api/...")], and ALWAYS an [Authorize(Policy = Policies.X)] (Bank.Modules.Auth.Policies:
  Customer, Staff, LoanOfficer, Compliance, Admin). Roles are in Bank.Modules.Auth.Roles.
- Current user: inject Bank.Modules.Auth.Application.ICurrentUser (UserId, IsInRole). Never trust user ids from request bodies.
- Time: inject Bank.Shared.Time.IClock and use clock.UtcNow. Never call DateTime.Now / DateTimeOffset.UtcNow directly.
- Settings: inject Bank.Shared.Settings.BankSettings (DefaultCurrency, SupportedCurrencies, DailyTransferLimit).
- Money: decimal only. Round to cents with Math.Round(x, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero).
- Money moves ONLY through Bank.Modules.Ledger.Application.ILedger.PostTransferAsync(from, to, amount, reference),
  which returns the transfer id and works in ONE currency. The bank's own accounts:
  Bank.Modules.Ledger.Domain.BankAccounts.For(BankAccountKind.X, currency) with kinds
  LoanFunding, FeeIncome, FxClearing, DepositHolding, InterestExpense, Suspense (EUR, SEK, USD).
- Accounts: Bank.Modules.Accounts.Application.IAccountService (GetAsync, IsOwnerAsync, ForOwnerAsync). Never edit Account.Balance.
- State machines: an entity owns its status (private setter) and changes it only through methods that check the current state.
- Entities: public parameterless constructor and public settable properties (except a status with a private setter),
  so the database and tests can create them.
- Events: sealed records implementing Bank.Shared.Events.IDomainEvent (with DateTimeOffset OccurredAt), published with
  IEventBus.PublishAsync(...). Handlers implement IEventHandler<TEvent> and are registered in their module.
- Personal data properties get [Pii] (Bank.Shared). Never put personal data in events, logs or messages.
- Do not name a type "Ledger" outside the Ledger module (namespace clash).
- Keep files small; one public type per file unless they are tiny records.


