# PR-11: Prevent duplicate payments on retries and concurrent requests via customer-scoped idempotency

**Story.** As a Nordbank customer using the mobile app, I want retried or concurrent payment requests with the same idempotency key to charge my account only once and return the original payment, so that network timeouts never cause me to be billed twice.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] Sequential retry with same key: When an authenticated customer calls the payment endpoint or IPaymentService.PayAsync multiple times with the same idempotency key, ILedger.PostTransferAsync is executed only once, only one payment record is persisted, the source account is debited once by the rounded decimal amount (Math.Round(amount, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero)), and every retry returns the first payment.
- [ ] Concurrent identical requests: When two or more requests with the same customer (ICurrentUser.UserId) and idempotency key arrive at the exact same moment, at most one transfer is posted through ILedger.PostTransferAsync, only one payment row exists in the database, and the concurrent call resolves by returning the persisted first payment rather than charging twice or failing with an unhandled 500 error.
- [ ] Database-level uniqueness: The payments table in BankDbContext defines a composite unique index on (CustomerId, IdempotencyKey), so the SQLite database rejects any duplicate insert for the same customer and idempotency key regardless of application-level race conditions.
- [ ] Customer isolation and authorization: Customer identity is taken exclusively from ICurrentUser.UserId (never from request payloads), source account ownership is verified via IAccountService.IsOwnerAsync, and controller authorization remains enforced via [Authorize(Policy = Policies.Customer)]. Two different customers submitting the same idempotency key are isolated from one another: each processes its own payment independently and neither can view or receive the other customer's payment.
- [ ] Atomic ledger and payment consistency: Money never moves via ILedger.PostTransferAsync if a duplicate key has already been claimed or fails the unique constraint; a failed or rejected duplicate attempt leaves account balances and ledger entries unchanged.
- [ ] Public contract preserved: IPaymentService.PayAsync and all existing public service and API signatures remain unchanged.
- [ ] Bank conventions and PII safety: Monetary values remain decimal rounded to cents away from zero, timestamps use IClock.UtcNow, and no personal data is written to logs or exception messages during duplicate-request handling.

## Out of scope
- Changing public signatures on IPaymentService or payment API request and response DTOs.
- Idempotency key expiration, TTL enforcement, or background cleanup jobs for historical keys.
- Detecting and rejecting differing request payloads (such as a different amount or recipient) reused with an existing idempotency key.
- Adding idempotency enforcement to non-payment modules or endpoints.
- Introducing external distributed caches or locks (such as Redis) or new NuGet packages.

## Risks
- Executing ILedger.PostTransferAsync before reserving or persisting the (CustomerId, IdempotencyKey) row can still double-charge the customer under a race condition even when a database unique index exists.
- Catching a unique constraint violation on a tracked DbContext without clearing or using a fresh query/scope can poison the EF Core change tracker and cause the retried request to return HTTP 500 instead of the original payment.
- Defining the unique index on IdempotencyKey alone without CustomerId could block legitimate payments from other customers or leak another customer's payment data.
- Tests using an in-memory provider instead of a real SQLite connection may miss SQLite unique constraint and write-locking behaviour under concurrent requests.

## Public contract
```
No public signature changes. IPaymentService.PayAsync keeps its behaviour; a repeated key returns the first payment.
```

## Allowed scope
- `src/Bank.Api/Modules/Payments/**`
- `src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs`

_Brief from the product owner:_ Support ticket #4711: a customer was charged twice for one payment after the mobile app retried a request that had timed out. Make sure a payment request with the same idempotency key from the same customer can never move money twice, also when two identical requests arrive at the same moment. The database must enforce it as well (a unique index on customer + idempotency key).


ALLOWED SCOPE (glob): ['src/Bank.Api/Modules/Payments/**', 'src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs']

CODING CONVENTIONS:
# Coding conventions for Nordbank (the Builder must follow these)

Stack: C# / ASP.NET Core Web API (.NET 8+), EF Core (SQLite), xUnit. No new NuGet packages.

## Layout — one folder per module, one folder per layer
src/Bank.Api/Modules/<Module>/
  Api/          controllers + DTOs            (HTTP layer)
  Application/  services + interfaces         (use cases)
  Domain/       entities, enums, events       (business rules)
  Data/         repositories (optional)       (database access)
  <Module>Module.cs   static class with AddXModule(this IServiceCollection): services.AddScoped<IThing, Thing>()
Register a new module in Program.cs above the "// MODULES:" line.
Add a new table as a DbSet in src/Bank.Api/Data/BankDbContext.cs above the "// TABLES:" line.
Each module already has a CONSTITUTION.md written by the humans who own it: follow its rules.

## Rules
- Namespaces follow folders: Bank.Modules.<Module>.<Layer>.
- Controllers: [ApiController], [Route("api/...")], and ALWAYS an [Authorize(Policy = Policies.X)] (Bank.Modules.Auth.Policies:
  Customer, Staff, LoanOfficer, Compliance, Admin). Roles are in Bank.Modules.Auth.Roles.
- Current user: inject Bank.Modules.Auth.Application.ICurrentUser (UserId, IsInRole). Never trust user ids from request bodies.
- Time: inject Bank.Shared.Time.IClock and use clock.UtcNow. Never call DateTime.Now / DateTimeOffset.UtcNow directly.
- Settings: inject Bank.Shared.Settings.BankSettings (DefaultCurrency, SupportedCurrencies, DailyTransferLimit).
- Money: decimal only. Round to cents with Math.Round(x, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero).
- Money moves ONLY through Bank.Modules.Ledger.Application.ILedger.PostTransferAsync(from, to, amount, reference),
  which returns the transfer id and works in ONE currency. The bank's own accounts:
  Bank.Modules.Ledger.Domain.BankAccounts.For(BankAccountKind.X, currency) with kinds
  LoanFunding, FeeIncome, FxClearing, DepositHolding, InterestExpense, Suspense (EUR, SEK, USD).
- Accounts: Bank.Modules.Accounts.Application.IAccountService (GetAsync, IsOwnerAsync, ForOwnerAsync). Never edit Account.Balance.
- State machines: an entity owns its status (private setter) and changes it only through methods that check the current state.
- Entities: public parameterless constructor and public settable properties (except a status with a private setter),
  so the database and tests can create them.
- Events: sealed records implementing Bank.Shared.Events.IDomainEvent (with DateTimeOffset OccurredAt), published with
  IEventBus.PublishAsync(...). Handlers implement IEventHandler<TEvent> and are registered in their module.
- Personal data properties get [Pii] (Bank.Shared). Never put personal data in events, logs or messages.
- Do not name a type "Ledger" outside the Ledger module (namespace clash).
- Keep files small; one public type per file unless they are tiny records.


