# PR-10: PR-10: Extract shared MoneyMath.ToCents helper and adopt across Fx, Fees, Deposits, and Loans

**Story.** As a Nordbank developer, I want a single shared MoneyMath.ToCents helper in Bank.Shared.Money used across the Fx, Fees, Deposits, and Loans modules so that all cent-rounding follows one consistent rule without changing any monetary amounts seen by customers.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] Create public static class MoneyMath in namespace Bank.Shared.Money (in its own file, no new NuGet packages) exposing public static decimal ToCents(decimal amount) implemented with Math.Round(amount, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero).
- [ ] Unit tests for MoneyMath.ToCents verify exact decimal outputs: 2.345m returns 2.35m, -2.345m returns -2.35m, 2.344m returns 2.34m, -2.344m returns -2.34m, 10.00m returns 10.00m, and 0m returns 0m.
- [ ] Replace all duplicated cent-rounding logic in the Fx, Fees, Deposits, and Loans modules with calls to MoneyMath.ToCents(decimal), leaving zero inline Math.Round(..., 2, ...) calls in those four modules.
- [ ] Preserve exact monetary behavior across Fx, Fees, Deposits, and Loans: for all existing test cases and boundary inputs (including half-cent values such as 2.345m and -2.345m), every calculated conversion, fee, deposit interest, and loan payment amount equals the pre-refactor amount to the exact cent, and all amounts passed to ILedger.PostTransferAsync remain rounded to 2 decimal places.
- [ ] Preserve all existing endpoint authorization policies ([Authorize(Policy = ...)] for Customer, Staff, LoanOfficer, Compliance, Admin), role checks, and HTTP error responses (such as 401/403 for unauthorized callers and 400 for invalid amounts) in Fx, Fees, Deposits, and Loans.

## Out of scope
- Changing the rounding strategy away from 2 decimal places with MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero or altering any customer-facing fee, FX, interest, or loan amounts.
- Introducing a Money value object, currency-specific decimal precision (e.g. 0 or 3 decimals), currency formatting, or additional MoneyMath methods beyond ToCents(decimal).
- Refactoring rounding logic in any module outside Fx, Fees, Deposits, and Loans.
- Modifying API endpoints, DTOs, database schemas, authorization policies, or adding NuGet packages.

## Risks
- If any existing module used default MidpointRounding.ToEven or truncation instead of AwayFromZero, switching to MoneyMath.ToCents would alter customer amounts on half-cent boundaries (e.g., 2.345 -> 2.35 instead of 2.34), breaking the zero-behavior-change guarantee.
- Missing a rounding call site or accidentally changing whether rounding occurs on intermediate vs. final amounts in Fx, Fees, Deposits, or Loans could cause 1-cent discrepancies, customer over/under-charging, or ledger transfer imbalances.
- Passing unrounded sub-cent decimal values into ILedger.PostTransferAsync if a rounding call is inadvertently removed during refactoring.
- Using float or double intermediates before calling MoneyMath.ToCents could introduce binary floating-point rounding errors.

## Public contract
```
namespace Bank.Shared.Money:
  static class MoneyMath { static decimal ToCents(decimal amount); }   // rounds to 2 decimals, half away from zero (2.345 -> 2.35, -2.345 -> -2.35)
