# PR-11: Prevent duplicate payments on retries and concurrent requests via customer-scoped idempotency

**Story.** As a Nordbank customer using the mobile app, I want retried or concurrent payment requests with the same idempotency key to charge my account only once and return the original payment, so that network timeouts never cause me to be billed twice.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] Sequential retry with same key: When an authenticated customer calls the payment endpoint or IPaymentService.PayAsync multiple times with the same idempotency key, ILedger.PostTransferAsync is executed only once, only one payment record is persisted, the source account is debited once by the rounded decimal amount (Math.Round(amount, 2, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero)), and every retry returns the first payment.
- [ ] Concurrent identical requests: When two or more requests with the same customer (ICurrentUser.UserId) and idempotency key arrive at the exact same moment, at most one transfer is posted through ILedger.PostTransferAsync, only one payment row exists in the database, and the concurrent call resolves by returning the persisted first payment rather than charging twice or failing with an unhandled 500 error.
- [ ] Database-level uniqueness: The payments table in BankDbContext defines a composite unique index on (CustomerId, IdempotencyKey), so the SQLite database rejects any duplicate insert for the same customer and idempotency key regardless of application-level race conditions.
- [ ] Customer isolation and authorization: Customer identity is taken exclusively from ICurrentUser.UserId (never from request payloads), source account ownership is verified via IAccountService.IsOwnerAsync, and controller authorization remains enforced via [Authorize(Policy = Policies.Customer)]. Two different customers submitting the same idempotency key are isolated from one another: each processes its own payment independently and neither can view or receive the other customer's payment.
- [ ] Atomic ledger and payment consistency: Money never moves via ILedger.PostTransferAsync if a duplicate key has already been claimed or fails the unique constraint; a failed or rejected duplicate attempt leaves account balances and ledger entries unchanged.
- [ ] Public contract preserved: IPaymentService.PayAsync and all existing public service and API signatures remain unchanged.
- [ ] Bank conventions and PII safety: Monetary values remain decimal rounded to cents away from zero, timestamps use IClock.UtcNow, and no personal data is written to logs or exception messages during duplicate-request handling.

## Out of scope
- Changing public signatures on IPaymentService or payment API request and response DTOs.
- Idempotency key expiration, TTL enforcement, or background cleanup jobs for historical keys.
- Detecting and rejecting differing request payloads (such as a different amount or recipient) reused with an existing idempotency key.
- Adding idempotency enforcement to non-payment modules or endpoints.
- Introducing external distributed caches or locks (such as Redis) or new NuGet packages.

## Risks
- Executing ILedger.PostTransferAsync before reserving or persisting the (CustomerId, IdempotencyKey) row can still double-charge the customer under a race condition even when a database unique index exists.
- Catching a unique constraint violation on a tracked DbContext without clearing or using a fresh query/scope can poison the EF Core change tracker and cause the retried 

